(function(){window.KPDF_ENGINE_DOCS={"api":"# API guide\n\nThe public API is organized by PDF responsibility. Start with high-level document, authoring, editing, and validation types. Use syntax and object types when you deliberately need the lower PDF layer.\n\n## Primary namespaces\n\n| Namespace | Use it for |\n| --- | --- |\n| `KillerPdf.Engine.Documents` | Open files, read document, page, text, bookmark, link, form, and tree information, run redaction, export, OCR, comparison, and other document workflows, and read native EPUB and DjVu snapshots |\n| `KillerPdf.Engine.Authoring` | Create documents, page content, resources, annotations, forms, navigation, and conformance structures |\n| `KillerPdf.Engine.Editing` | Append byte-preserving revisions to existing documents |\n| `KillerPdf.Engine.Writing` | Deterministic rewrites, incremental update options, and output sanitization |\n| `KillerPdf.Engine.Diagnostics` | Bounded structural inspection and diagnostics |\n| `KillerPdf.Engine.Validation` | Deterministic reopen and round-trip verification |\n| `KillerPdf.Engine.Security` | Password authentication, permissions, encryption options, and security handlers |\n| `KillerPdf.Engine.Signing` | Signature creation, discovery, verification, certification, and revision analysis |\n| `KillerPdf.Engine.Rendering` | Render pages to BGRA32 pixel buffers or float32 frames, with printer proof options and gamut masks |\n| `KillerPdf.Engine.ColorManagement` | Load ICC profiles, convert color between profiles, describe printer proofs and presets, check printer gamut, and supply the bundled working CMYK profile |\n| `KillerPdf.Engine.Formats.Epub` | Open EPUB packages, read metadata, text, and navigation, lay out and render pages, and convert books to PDF |\n| `KillerPdf.Engine.Formats.Djvu` | Open DjVu documents, read page information, text, and navigation, render pages, and convert documents to PDF |\n| `KillerPdf.Engine.Fonts` | Font loading, text codes, glyph outlines, and host font resolvers |\n| `KillerPdf.Engine.Objects` | Typed PDF objects for lower-level work |\n| `KillerPdf.Engine.Syntax` | Headers, tokens, tokenizer behavior, and PDF versions |\n\n## Common entry points\n\n| Task | Start with |\n| --- | --- |\n| Open a PDF | `PdfDocument.Open` |\n| Inspect unknown input | `PdfDocumentInspector.Inspect` |\n| Read metadata and page count | `PdfDocumentInformation.Read` |\n| Read page dimensions and rotation | `PdfPageInformation.Read` |\n| Read bookmarks | `PdfBookmarkReader` |\n| Read links | `PdfLinkReader` |\n| Read forms | `PdfFormWidgetReader` |\n| Extract text and geometry | `PdfPageContentReader` |\n| Render a page | `PdfPageRenderer` |\n| Render with float32 precision | `PdfPageRenderer.RenderFrame` and `PdfPageRenderer.RenderFrameRegion` |\n| Proof a render for a printer | `PdfRenderOptions.Proof`, `PdfDocumentProofSettings`, and `PdfProofPreset` |\n| Mark out-of-gamut pixels | `PdfRenderedFrame.WithGamutWarningsMarked` |\n| Load the working CMYK profile | `PdfCmykViewingDefault.LoadProfile` |\n| Open an EPUB | `EpubDocument.Open`, `PdfReadSnapshot.OpenEpub`, and `PdfReadSnapshot.OpenEpubProgressive` |\n| Open a DjVu document | `DjvuDocument.Open` and `PdfReadSnapshot.OpenDjvu` |\n| Convert EPUB or DjVu to PDF | `EpubDocument.ConvertToPdf` and `DjvuDocument.ConvertToPdf` |\n| Redact content | `PdfRedactionSearch` and `PdfContentRedaction` |\n| Optimize a file | `PdfOptimizer` |\n| Export to Office, HTML, Markdown, or JSON | `PdfStructuredExport` |\n| Run preflight | `PdfPreflightRunner` |\n| Create a PDF | `PdfDocumentBuilder` |\n| Build page content | `PdfContentStreamBuilder` |\n| Change pages and forms | `PdfIncrementalPageEditor` |\n| Change annotations and links | `PdfIncrementalAnnotationEditor` |\n| Move, resize, or remove a placed image | `PdfImagePlacementEditor` |\n| Select or extract pixels | `PdfRasterSelection` and `PdfRasterExtraction` |\n| Rewrite deterministically | `PdfDocumentWriter.Write` |\n| Validate a rewrite | `PdfRoundTripValidator.Validate` |\n| Read and verify signatures | `PdfSignatureReader`, `PdfSignatureVerifier`, and `PdfSignatureInspection` |\n\n## High-level before low-level\n\nThe high-level APIs coordinate changes across related PDF structures. For example, adding a form field can affect the page annotations, AcroForm field tree, appearance resources, default resources, and document catalog. Prefer the typed method that represents your intent.\n\nUse `PdfObject`, `PdfDictionary`, `PdfArray`, `PdfStream`, `PdfName`, and indirect references when implementing a feature that the high-level surface does not cover. Validate the complete graph before writing.\n\n## API documentation in your IDE\n\nThe package includes XML documentation generated from the public source comments. Visual Studio, Rider, and other .NET editors display summaries and parameter help while you type.\n\nFor the complete current surface, browse the [engine source](https://github.com/SteveTheKiller/KillerPDF/tree/main/engine/KillerPdf.Engine) or inspect the package with your IDE's object browser.\n\n## Versioning\n\nThe package version follows the KillerPDF release that ships it. Pin the version your application has tested. Review the [engine changelog](https://github.com/SteveTheKiller/KillerPDF/blob/main/engine/CHANGELOG.md) before upgrading.\n","architecture":"# Architecture\n\nThe KillerPDF.Engine is a reusable document library inside the KillerPDF monorepo. The project reference enforces a UI-free boundary while the shared repository lets application integration, library behavior, tests, and corpus gates evolve together.\n\n## Library boundary\n\nKillerPDF 1.9 uses The KillerPDF.Engine for rendering, text extraction, and OCR. Tesseract remains available in the desktop app as a fallback OCR provider. See the developer guide for current engine APIs.\n\n## Internal layers\n\n| Layer | Responsibility |\n| --- | --- |\n| `Syntax` and `Objects` | Binary-safe tokens and the typed PDF object model |\n| `CrossReference`, `Filters`, and `Documents` | Revision discovery, stream decoding, lazy resolution, and authenticated access |\n| `Authoring` | Typed construction of pages, content, resources, metadata, interactive features, and standards structures |\n| `Editing` | High-level byte-preserving changes and safe cross-document graph imports |\n| `Writing` | Incremental revisions, deterministic rewrites, metadata policy, and output sanitization |\n| `Security` and `Signing` | Authentication, permissions, password and certificate encryption, signatures, timestamps, and revision verification |\n| `Rendering` and `Fonts` | BGRA32 and float32 page rendering, image decoding, font decoding, and glyph outlines |\n| `ColorManagement` | ICC profile loading, rendering intents, printer proof settings and presets, printer gamut checks, and the bundled working CMYK profile |\n| `Formats` | Native EPUB and DjVu readers, which `PdfReadSnapshot` in `Documents` presents through one read surface and which convert to PDF through `Authoring` |\n| `Diagnostics` and `Validation` | Bounded inspection, implementation limits, structural reports, and round-trip checks |\n\n## Read flow\n\n1. Parse the header and locate the final `startxref` marker.\n2. Read the cross-reference revision chain.\n3. Authenticate encrypted documents before resolving protected objects.\n4. Resolve indirect objects lazily through the cross-reference table.\n5. Present common document features through typed readers.\n\n## Write flow\n\nFor a deterministic rewrite, `PdfDocumentWriter` walks the resolved graph and serializes a normalized document. Repeating the write with equivalent input produces reproducible output.\n\nFor an incremental edit, a high-level editor records the intended changes, imports dependent graphs when needed, validates the result, and appends one new revision without modifying the original byte prefix.\n\n## Native format flow\n\nEPUB and DjVu documents do not pass through the PDF object model. Their own readers parse the package or container, and `PdfReadSnapshot` exposes pages, text, outlines, and rendering for them. A PDF exists only when you call a `ConvertToPdf` method, which builds a separate document with `PdfDocumentBuilder`.\n\nContent a reader cannot represent is reported through diagnostics, or the reader refuses it.\n\n## Fail-closed editing\n\nSome operations intentionally refuse incomplete global structures. Examples include unsafe partial imports from tagged documents, ambiguous shared object graphs, unsupported XFA merges, missing credentials, and edits forbidden by authenticated user permissions.\n\nA refusal is safer than producing a file that opens but has silently damaged navigation, forms, accessibility, optional content, or signatures.\n\n## Architecture decisions\n\n- [ADR-001: PDF engine boundary](https://github.com/SteveTheKiller/KillerPDF/blob/main/engine/docs/architecture/ADR-001-pdf-engine-boundary.md)\n- [ADR-002: Modern Windows shell integration](https://github.com/SteveTheKiller/KillerPDF/blob/main/engine/docs/architecture/ADR-002-integrate-engine-through-modern-windows-shell.md)\n- [ADR-003: Installed and portable packages](https://github.com/SteveTheKiller/KillerPDF/blob/main/engine/docs/architecture/ADR-003-split-installed-and-portable-packages.md)\n","creating-pdfs":"# Creating PDFs\n\nUse `PdfDocumentBuilder` and `PdfContentStreamBuilder` to create typed PDF 2.0 documents without assembling raw object dictionaries.\n\n## Create pages and metadata\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Authoring;\n\nvar builder = new PdfDocumentBuilder()\n    .SetMetadata(new PdfDocumentMetadata\n    {\n        Title = \"Quarterly report\",\n        Author = \"Example application\",\n        Language = \"en-US\"\n    })\n    .AddBlankPage(612, 792);\n\nbyte[] pdf = builder.Build();\n```\n\n`Build` validates coordinated document features before serialization. Invalid page geometry, incomplete standard structures, and inconsistent field definitions fail before a file is written.\n\n## Write page content\n\n```csharp\nvar content = new PdfContentStreamBuilder()\n    .BeginText()\n    .SetFont(PdfStandardFont.Helvetica, 18)\n    .MoveText(72, 720)\n    .ShowLatin1Text(\"Created with The KillerPDF.Engine\")\n    .EndText();\n\nbyte[] pdf = new PdfDocumentBuilder()\n    .AddPage(612, 792, content)\n    .Build();\n```\n\nThe content builder also supports paths, line styles, colors, images, embedded TrueType fonts, transparency, graphics state, patterns, shadings, optional content, and tagged marked content.\n\n## Add document features\n\nThe same builder coordinates features that otherwise require changes across the page tree, catalog, resources, name trees, structure tree, and AcroForm:\n\n- Bookmarks, destinations, page labels, and viewer preferences\n- Links, attachments, annotations, replies, and popups\n- Text fields, checkboxes, radio buttons, choice fields, push buttons, and signature fields\n- Output intents, embedded files, optional content groups, and structure elements\n\nUse the typed methods on `PdfDocumentBuilder`. Avoid dropping to raw objects unless your application is implementing a PDF feature that the high-level API does not yet expose.\n\n## Author for PDF/A and PDF/UA\n\nThe builder provides coordinated safeguards for PDF/A-4, PDF/A-4e, PDF/A-4f, and PDF/UA-2:\n\n```csharp\nvar builder = new PdfDocumentBuilder()\n    .EnablePdfA4Conformance()\n    .EnablePdfUa2Conformance();\n```\n\nConformance requires more than these calls. Your document still needs suitable metadata, output intents, embedded fonts, semantic structure, language information, and accessible content. Validate the finished file with an independent validator.\n\n## Save the result\n\n```csharp\nFile.WriteAllBytes(\"created.pdf\", builder.Build());\n```\n\nBuild into memory first, then commit the finished byte array to your storage layer. This prevents a failed build from leaving a partial PDF on disk.\n","editing-pdfs":"# Editing PDFs\n\nUse the incremental editors to change an existing document while retaining its original byte prefix and adding one bounded revision.\n\n## Why incremental editing matters\n\nAn incremental update appends changed objects, a new cross-reference section, and a new trailer. The original bytes remain intact. This is important for preservation-sensitive workflows, audit trails, and signed revision analysis.\n\nUse a deterministic full rewrite when you intentionally want a normalized document instead.\n\n## Change pages\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Documents;\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Editing;\n\nPdfDocument document = PdfDocument.Open(File.ReadAllBytes(\"input.pdf\"));\n\nbyte[] updated = new PdfIncrementalPageEditor(document)\n    .RotateClockwise(0)\n    .MovePage(2, 0)\n    .AddBlankPage(612, 792)\n    .Build();\n\nFile.WriteAllBytes(\"updated.pdf\", updated);\n```\n\nPage operations include insertion, import, removal, reordering, rotation, page boxes, user units, transitions, thumbnails, content replacement, and content appending.\n\n## Copy a page from another PDF\n\n`AddImportedPage` copies the selected page and its dependent object graph into the destination document. If either document contains an AcroForm, the editor also merges the form field tree, renames colliding resource names in `/DR`, and rewrites affected `/DA` strings.\n\n```csharp\nbyte[] destinationBytes = File.ReadAllBytes(\"destination.pdf\");\nbyte[] sourceBytes = File.ReadAllBytes(\"source.pdf\");\n\nPdfDocument destination = PdfDocument.Open(destinationBytes);\nPdfDocument source = PdfDocument.Open(sourceBytes);\n\nbyte[] updated = new PdfIncrementalPageEditor(destination)\n    .AddImportedPage(source, sourcePageIndex: 0)\n    .Build();\n\nFile.WriteAllBytes(\"combined.pdf\", updated);\n```\n\nPage indexes are zero-based. Duplicate fully qualified form field names throw an exception instead of being renamed automatically.\n\n## Insert an image at a page position\n\nUse `PdfContentStreamBuilder` to place an image directly into a page's content. PDF coordinates start at the lower-left corner of the page.\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Authoring;\n\nPdfPageInformation page = PdfPageInformation.Read(document)[0];\nPdfImage image = PdfImage.FromJpeg(File.ReadAllBytes(\"photo.jpg\"));\n\nvar content = new PdfContentStreamBuilder()\n    .DrawImage(image, x: 72, y: 500, width: 180, height: 120);\n\nbyte[] updated = new PdfIncrementalPageEditor(document)\n    .AppendPageContent(0, page.Width, page.Height, content)\n    .Build();\n\nFile.WriteAllBytes(\"with-image.pdf\", updated);\n```\n\nFor an image that should remain an annotation, use `PdfIncrementalAnnotationEditor.AddImageStamp` with the same page, position, and dimensions.\n\n## Move, resize, or remove a placed image\n\n`PdfPageContentReader.ReadImagePlacements` returns one handle for each image occurrence on a page. `PdfImagePlacementEditor` then moves, resizes, or removes the occurrence in a detached incremental revision. It preserves shared resources and unrelated content.\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Documents;\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Editing;\n\nvar reader = new PdfPageContentReader(document);\nPdfImagePlacementSelection image = reader.ReadImagePlacements(0)[0];\n\nif (image.CanSetBounds)\n{\n    var target = new PdfContentBounds(72, 400, 252, 520);\n    byte[] moved = PdfImagePlacementEditor.SetBounds(document, image, target);\n}\n\nif (image.CanRemove)\n{\n    byte[] without = PdfImagePlacementEditor.Remove(document, image);\n}\n```\n\nA handle belongs to the `PdfDocument` instance that read it, and the editor throws `ArgumentException` for any other instance. `SetBounds` takes the new rectangle as a `PdfContentBounds` in unrotated, crop-relative PDF points, and it needs a placement that is axis-aligned, unclipped, and valid. `CanSetBounds` and `CanRemove` report whether an occurrence is supported, and `BoundsUnsupportedReason` and `UnsupportedReason` give a stable reason when it is not. Removal honors the document's modification, certification, and structure restrictions.\n\n## Extract an image as a detached asset\n\n`PdfImagePlacementExtractor.Extract` copies one original image into a `PdfImageAsset`, an immutable single-image resource that does not belong to the source document. It keeps the image's pixels, an RGB ICC profile and rendering intent, and a matching soft mask.\n\n```csharp\nPdfImageAsset asset = PdfImagePlacementExtractor.Extract(document, image);\nConsole.WriteLine($\"{asset.Width} x {asset.Height}\");\n\nbyte[] assetPage = new PdfDocumentBuilder().AddPage(200, 150,\n    new PdfContentStreamBuilder().DrawImage(asset, 0, 0, 200, 150)).Build();\n\nbyte[] pasted = new PdfIncrementalPageEditor(document)\n    .AppendPageContent(0, 612, 792, new PdfContentStreamBuilder().DrawImage(asset, 10, 10, 100, 100))\n    .Build();\n```\n\n`PdfContentStreamBuilder.DrawImage` places an asset on a new page or appends it to an existing page, and `PdfImageAsset.ToPdfBytes` returns it as a PDF with a page the size of the image. The extractor handles one raw or Flate image, or a JPEG with direct DeviceRGB color, with eight bits per component. An image with a color space the asset cannot carry, a property it does not preserve such as a Decode array or an image mask, or a JPEG with a soft mask throws `NotSupportedException`. An asset, its soft mask, and its encoded data are limited to 5 MiB. A document opened with a user password must allow content copying, or the extractor throws `InvalidOperationException`.\n\n## Select and extract pixels from a render\n\n`PdfRasterSelection` and `PdfRasterExtraction` work on a rendered float frame, so they can pick up content that is not a placed image, such as a drawing or part of a scan. A selection is an immutable mask of whole pixels with a top-left origin, and each pixel is either selected or not.\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Rendering;\n\nvar renderer = new PdfPageRenderer(document);\nPdfRenderedFrame frame = renderer.RenderFrame(0, new PdfRenderOptions(612, 792));\n\nPdfRasterSelection area = PdfRasterSelection.SelectRectangle(\n    frame.Width, frame.Height, left: 100, top: 100, selectionWidth: 300, selectionHeight: 200);\nPdfRasterSelection color = PdfRasterSelection.SelectConnected(frame, seedX: 120, seedY: 130, tolerance: 0.05f);\n\nPdfRasterSelection both = area.Add(color);\nPdfRasterSelection withoutColor = area.Subtract(color);\n\nif (PdfRasterExtraction.AllowsExtraction(document))\n{\n    PdfRasterExtractionResult result = PdfRasterExtraction.Extract(frame, both, featherRadius: 2);\n    Console.WriteLine($\"{result.Width} x {result.Height} at {result.Left}, {result.Top}\");\n\n    var (red, green, blue) = PdfRasterExtraction.SampleFillColor(frame, both);\n    PdfImageAsset fill = PdfRasterExtraction.CreateFill(both, result, red, green, blue);\n}\n```\n\n`SelectConnected` selects the four-connected region of visible pixels whose RGB values are within `tolerance` of the seed pixel. The tolerance is the largest allowed difference in any channel, it is inclusive, and it uses the frame's own channel encoding. A seed pixel with zero alpha selects nothing. `SelectRectangle` selects a rectangle inside the frame. `Add` and `Subtract` return a new selection with the same dimensions, so you can refine a selection and leave transparent holes. Each operation takes a cancellation token and an optional `maximumWorkingBytes` limit of at most 256 MiB, which is also the default, and it throws `ArgumentException` when the work would not fit.\n\n`Extract` copies the selected pixels into a transparent `PdfImageAsset` that covers the selection's bounding box. `PdfRasterExtractionResult.Left`, `Top`, `Width`, and `Height` give that rectangle in frame pixels. A `featherRadius` from 0 to `PdfRasterExtraction.MaximumFeatherRadius` (32) softens the edge of the mask, and 0 keeps hard edges. Pixels come from the frame's own sRGB output and never from a monitor or proof view, and a transparent source pixel stays transparent. An empty selection, or one with dimensions different from the frame, throws `ArgumentException`. Like an extracted page picture, the result is limited to `PdfRasterExtraction.MaximumPixels` (1,310,720 pixels) and 5 MiB of compressed data, and a larger area throws `NotSupportedException`.\n\nTo cut an area instead of copying it, `SampleFillColor` takes the mean sRGB color of the opaque pixels a fixed distance outside the selection (`DefaultFillRingDistance`, 3 pixels), and `CreateFill` builds a solid image in the selection's shape that covers the cut area. `Extract` does not check the document's permissions itself, so call `AllowsExtraction` first. It returns false when the document was opened with a user password that does not allow content copying.\n\n## Update document features\n\n`PdfIncrementalPageEditor` also changes metadata, output intents, page layout, viewer preferences, open actions, named destinations, page labels, attachments, bookmarks, and AcroForm fields.\n\n```csharp\nbyte[] updated = new PdfIncrementalPageEditor(document)\n    .SetTextFieldValue(\"Customer.Name\", \"Ada Lovelace\")\n    .SetCheckBoxValue(\"Terms.Accepted\", true)\n    .Build();\n```\n\n## Edit annotations\n\nUse `PdfIncrementalAnnotationEditor` for page annotations and links:\n\n```csharp\nvar editor = new PdfIncrementalAnnotationEditor(document);\n\nbyte[] updated = editor\n    .AddTextNote(0, 72, 700, \"Reviewed\")\n    .Build();\n```\n\nThe annotation editor supports text notes, text markup, free text, lines, shapes, ink, image stamps, redaction marks, file attachments, and links.\n\n## Remove, rewrite, and optimize\n\nSome edits change content rather than add a revision on top of it:\n\n| Task | Start with |\n| --- | --- |\n| Permanently remove text, images, annotations, and fields under reviewed areas, then verify the result | `PdfRedactionSearch` and `PdfContentRedaction` |\n| Rewrite a line of page text in place | `PdfTextLineEditor` |\n| Preview and apply lossless optimization or selective removal | `PdfOptimizer` |\n| Flatten form fields | `PdfFormFlattener` |\n| Export to HTML, Markdown, JSON, Word, Excel, or PowerPoint formats | `PdfStructuredExport` |\n\nRedaction and optimization write a new full rewrite, so they invalidate existing signatures unless you plan for that. Incremental edits keep earlier revisions in the file, so they are not a way to erase data.\n\n## Import safely\n\nPage imports copy the complete dependent object graph with collision handling. The editor refuses imports that depend on unsupported global state rather than producing a document with silently damaged forms, navigation, optional content, or accessibility structure.\n\n## Write to a new destination\n\nKeep the source and output paths separate until `Build` succeeds. Replace the original file only after your application has completed its own validation and durable write procedure.\n","getting-started":"# Getting started\n\nInstall The KillerPDF.Engine, create a PDF 2.0 document, and reopen it in less than five minutes.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- .NET 10 SDK\n- A .NET console, desktop, web, or service project\n- Windows, Linux, or macOS for the engine itself\n\nThe library is UI-free. Your application chooses its own renderer, interface, storage, and deployment model.\n\n## Install from NuGet\n\nRun this in your project directory:\n\n```powershell\ndotnet add package KillerPdf.Engine\n```\n\nWhen working from a KillerPDF repository checkout, use a project reference instead:\n\n```xml\n<ProjectReference Include=\"path\\to\\KillerPDF\\engine\\KillerPdf.Engine\\KillerPdf.Engine.csproj\" />\n```\n\n## Create your first document\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Authoring;\n\nbyte[] pdf = new PdfDocumentBuilder()\n    .SetMetadata(new PdfDocumentMetadata\n    {\n        Title = \"Hello from The KillerPDF.Engine\",\n        Author = \"Example application\",\n        Language = \"en-US\"\n    })\n    .AddBlankPage(612, 792)\n    .Build();\n\nFile.WriteAllBytes(\"hello.pdf\", pdf);\n```\n\nPage dimensions use PDF points. A US Letter page is 612 by 792 points.\n\n## Open and inspect it\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Documents;\n\nbyte[] source = File.ReadAllBytes(\"hello.pdf\");\nPdfDocument document = PdfDocument.Open(source);\nPdfDocumentInformation information = PdfDocumentInformation.Read(document);\n\nConsole.WriteLine(information.Title);\nConsole.WriteLine($\"{information.PageCount} page(s)\");\nConsole.WriteLine($\"PDF {information.Version}\");\n```\n\n## Rewrite it deterministically\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Writing;\n\nbyte[] rewritten = PdfDocumentWriter.Write(document);\nFile.WriteAllBytes(\"hello-rewritten.pdf\", rewritten);\n```\n\nUse a deterministic rewrite when reproducible output and a normalized object graph matter. Use the incremental editors when preserving the original byte prefix matters.\n\n## Choose the next guide\n\n- [Reading PDFs](reading-pdfs.html) covers metadata, pages, links, bookmarks, and forms.\n- [Creating PDFs](creating-pdfs.html) covers pages, content streams, metadata, and modern standards.\n- [Editing PDFs](editing-pdfs.html) covers byte-preserving changes to existing files.\n- [Validation](validation.html) covers structural inspection and independent validators.\n","overview":"# The KillerPDF.Engine\n\nThe KillerPDF.Engine is an independent, UI-free .NET 10 library for reading, extracting text, rendering, searching, validating, authoring, editing, redacting, signing, encrypting, and writing PDF files. It also reads EPUB and DjVu documents natively, converts them to PDF, and renders with ICC color management and printer proofing. It gives applications a modern PDF 2.0, PDF/A, and PDF/UA foundation without tying them to the KillerPDF desktop interface.\n\n## Start building\n\nInstall the package from [NuGet.org](https://www.nuget.org/packages/KillerPdf.Engine):\n\n```powershell\ndotnet add package KillerPdf.Engine\n```\n\n[Getting started](getting-started.html)\n\nUse the task guides in the sidebar when you already know what you need to do.\n\n## What the engine covers\n\n- PDF syntax, objects, streams, cross-reference tables, cross-reference streams, object streams, trailers, and incremental revisions\n- Deterministic full rewrites and byte-preserving incremental updates\n- PDF 2.0 authoring with pages, content streams, fonts, images, color spaces, patterns, transparency, and resources\n- Bookmarks, named destinations, page labels, links, attachments, annotations, optional content, and AcroForm fields\n- Tagged PDF, PDF/UA-2, PDF/A-4, PDF/A-4e, and PDF/A-4f authoring safeguards\n- RC4, AES-128, AES-256, and AES-GCM password security, and certificate-recipient encryption\n- Detached CMS signatures, certification permissions, field locks, RFC 3161 timestamps, verification with trust and revocation policy, PAdES validation evidence, and signed revision analysis\n- Page text, word, and table extraction, and bounded BGRA32 page rendering without a native renderer\n- OCR with local models, searchable text layers, and reviewed corrections\n- Content-level redaction with sensitive-data search and read-back verification\n- AcroForm editing, FDF and XFDF interchange, XFA inspection and conversion, and data merge\n- Export to plain text, HTML, Markdown, JSON, Word, Excel, and PowerPoint formats\n- Native EPUB and DjVu reading (in development): metadata, text, outlines, page and region rendering, explicit conversion to PDF, and link regions for EPUB\n- ICC color management: float32 page rendering, monitor-profile output, a bundled working CMYK profile, and printer proofing with all four rendering intents, black-point compensation, paper and black simulation, overprint simulation, and gamut warnings. HDR output and physical color accuracy are still in progress\n- Picture editing: move, resize, and remove placed images, detached image assets, and rectangle or color-area pixel selection and extraction\n- Repair and lossless optimization plans, preflight, accessibility, and print-production reports\n- Imposition, headers, footers, Bates numbering, calibrated measurement, layers, attachments, and structural comparison\n- Structural diagnostics, bounded parsing, implementation limits, and round-trip validation\n\n## Project scale\n\n| Measure | Current 1.9.0 development tree |\n| --- | --- |\n| Engine source files | 386 |\n| Physical C# lines | 130,218 |\n| Engine tests | 6,427 |\n| Corpus PDFs | 47,024 |\n\n## A real library boundary\n\nKillerPDF 1.9 uses The KillerPDF.Engine for rendering, text extraction, and OCR. Tesseract remains available in the desktop app as a fallback OCR provider. See the developer guide for current engine APIs.\n\nThe monorepo keeps the engine, application integration, tests, and corpus gates in one reviewable change. The project boundary still enforces a reusable library that other .NET applications can reference through NuGet.\n\n## Design principles\n\n- Preserve existing bytes when an operation can be represented as an incremental revision.\n- Fail closed when required structure cannot be interpreted or preserved safely.\n- Emit deterministic output so regressions are reproducible.\n- Enforce explicit implementation limits before allocating or serializing unbounded structures.\n- Keep public APIs typed instead of exposing KillerPDF application state.\n- Treat conformance as validator-backed behavior, not a label inferred from the PDF header.\n\n## Source and license\n\nThe engine is developed in the [KillerPDF repository](https://github.com/SteveTheKiller/KillerPDF/tree/main/engine) and licensed under GPLv3. Detailed capability history is recorded in the [engine changelog](https://github.com/SteveTheKiller/KillerPDF/blob/main/engine/CHANGELOG.md).\n","reading-pdfs":"# Reading PDFs\n\nOpen documents from memory, inspect unfamiliar input, and read high-level document features without exposing parser internals to the rest of your application.\n\n## Open a document\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Documents;\n\nbyte[] source = File.ReadAllBytes(\"input.pdf\");\nPdfDocument document = PdfDocument.Open(source);\n```\n\nFor an encrypted document, pass the user or owner password:\n\n```csharp\nPdfDocument document = PdfDocument.Open(source, password);\n```\n\nThe document keeps its own copy of the supplied bytes, so your buffer can be reused after the call. Use `PdfDocument.OpenWithCompatibilityRecovery` when a viewer needs bounded recovery from a damaged file. Strict parsing remains the default.\n\n## Inspect before opening\n\nUse the non-throwing structural inspector when a file came from an unfamiliar or untrusted source:\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Diagnostics;\n\nPdfInspectionReport report = PdfDocumentInspector.Inspect(source);\nif (!report.IsStructurallyValid)\n{\n    foreach (PdfDiagnostic diagnostic in report.Diagnostics)\n        Console.WriteLine($\"{diagnostic.Code}: {diagnostic.Message}\");\n}\n```\n\nEncrypted input reports that authentication is required. Use `InspectAuthenticated` when your application has a password.\n\n## Read document information\n\n```csharp\nPdfDocumentInformation information = PdfDocumentInformation.Read(document);\n\nConsole.WriteLine(information.Title);\nConsole.WriteLine(information.Author);\nConsole.WriteLine(information.Language);\nConsole.WriteLine(information.PageCount);\n```\n\n## Read page geometry\n\n```csharp\nIReadOnlyList<PdfPageInformation> pages = PdfPageInformation.Read(document);\n\nfor (int index = 0; index < pages.Count; index++)\n{\n    PdfPageInformation page = pages[index];\n    Console.WriteLine($\"Page {index + 1}: {page.Width} x {page.Height}, {page.Rotation} degrees\");\n}\n```\n\nThe effective geometry accounts for inherited page boxes and rotation.\n\n## Read interactive features\n\n| Feature | Reader |\n| --- | --- |\n| Bookmarks | `PdfBookmarkReader` |\n| Links | `PdfLinkReader` |\n| Form widgets | `PdfFormWidgetReader` |\n| Signatures | `PdfSignatureReader` |\n| Attachments | `PdfAttachmentReader` |\n| Comments | `PdfCommentReader` |\n| Layers | `PdfOptionalContentReader` |\n\nThese readers return stable, high-level records with decoded text and normalized geometry. Your application does not need to traverse raw dictionaries for common tasks.\n\n## Read form fields\n\nForm widgets are read one page at a time. Each result includes the fully qualified field name, field type, current value, flags, options, and normalized page coordinates.\n\n```csharp\nIReadOnlyList<PdfPageInformation> pages = PdfPageInformation.Read(document);\n\nfor (int pageIndex = 0; pageIndex < pages.Count; pageIndex++)\n{\n    foreach (PdfFormWidgetInfo widget in PdfFormWidgetReader.ReadPage(document, pageIndex))\n    {\n        Console.WriteLine($\"{widget.FieldName}: {widget.Value}\");\n        Console.WriteLine($\"  Type: {widget.FieldKind}\");\n        Console.WriteLine($\"  Rectangle: {widget.Left}, {widget.Bottom}, {widget.Right}, {widget.Top}\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\nAn empty page returns an empty list. Page indexes are zero-based.\n\n## Extract text and render pages\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Documents;\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Rendering;\n\nvar reader = new PdfPageContentReader(document);\nPdfPageContent content = reader.Read(0);\nforeach (PdfExtractedWord word in content.Words)\n    Console.WriteLine($\"{word.Text}: {word.BoundingBox}\");\n\nvar renderer = new PdfPageRenderer(document);\nPdfRenderedPage image = renderer.Render(0, new PdfRenderOptions(1024, 768));\n```\n\nExtraction returns words, text runs, lines, glyph bounds, fonts, images, paths, and shadings in unrotated, crop-relative PDF points. Rendering returns tightly packed BGRA32 pixels with a top-left origin, and the caller chooses how to display or encode them. Both report diagnostics for any recovery they applied.\n\n## Read EPUB and DjVu documents\n\nEPUB and DjVu support is in development. The engine reads both formats with its own readers and does not convert them to PDF unless you ask. `PdfReadSnapshot` presents either format through one surface for page count, page geometry, text, outline, and rendering.\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Documents;\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Fonts;\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Formats.Epub;\n\nTrueTypeFont font = TrueTypeFont.Load(File.ReadAllBytes(\"regular.ttf\"));\nvar options = new EpubLayoutOptions(PageWidth: 794, PageHeight: 1123, DefaultFont: font);\n\nPdfReadSnapshot book = PdfReadSnapshot.OpenEpub(File.ReadAllBytes(\"book.epub\"), options);\nPdfReadSnapshot scan = PdfReadSnapshot.OpenDjvu(File.ReadAllBytes(\"scan.djvu\"));\n\nConsole.WriteLine($\"{book.Format}: {book.PageCount} pages\");\nConsole.WriteLine($\"{scan.Format}: {scan.PageCount} pages\");\n\nPdfReadPageGeometry geometry = scan.GetPageGeometry(0);\nConsole.WriteLine($\"{geometry.Width} x {geometry.Height} {geometry.Unit} at {geometry.DpiX} dpi\");\n```\n\nEPUB is reflowable, so layout needs a page size and a fallback TrueType font that your application supplies. `EpubLayoutOptions` takes the page size in CSS pixels at 96 per inch, and every page of one book shares that geometry. A DjVu page is a scan, so each page reports its own size in pixels and its recorded resolution. `PdfReadSnapshot.OpenEpub` lays out the whole book before it returns. The snapshot keeps its own copy of the bytes, and the `Owned` variants take your array instead of copying it.\n\nFor metadata, the reading order, or raw chapter text without a layout, open the format directly:\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Formats.Djvu;\n\nEpubDocument epub = EpubDocument.Open(File.ReadAllBytes(\"book.epub\"));\nConsole.WriteLine($\"{epub.Metadata.Title} by {epub.Metadata.Creator}\");\nforeach (EpubSpineItem item in epub.Spine)\n    Console.WriteLine(item.Item.Href);\nstring firstChapter = epub.ReadText(0);\n\nDjvuDocument djvu = DjvuDocument.Open(File.ReadAllBytes(\"scan.djvu\"));\nforeach (DjvuDiagnostic notice in djvu.Diagnostics)\n    Console.WriteLine($\"{notice.Code}: {notice.Message}\");\n```\n\n`EpubDocument` accepts EPUB 2 and EPUB 3 packages. `DjvuDocument.Pages` lists each page's size, resolution, orientation, and bundled component name. Opening a DjVu document does not decode any page image.\n\n## Open a long EPUB progressively\n\n`PdfReadSnapshot.OpenEpubProgressive` lays out the first chapter in reading order and returns while a background thread paginates the rest of the book. `PageCount` grows by whole chapters, pages already counted never change, and the finished snapshot has the same pages that `OpenEpub` lays out.\n\n```csharp\nPdfReadSnapshot book = PdfReadSnapshot.OpenEpubProgressive(bytes, options);\ntry\n{\n    bool exists = await book.WaitForPageAsync(40);\n\n    string target = \"EPUB/chapter12.xhtml#start\";\n    await book.WaitForNavigationTargetAsync(target);\n    PdfReadNavigationDestination place = book.ResolveNavigationTarget(target);\n\n    await book.Pagination;\n    Console.WriteLine($\"{book.PageCount} pages\");\n}\nfinally\n{\n    book.ReleaseLayout();\n}\n```\n\n`IsPaginating` and `PaginationProgress` report how far pagination has come. `WaitForPageAsync` returns false when pagination ended before it reached the page. `ResolveNavigationTarget` throws `InvalidOperationException` for a target whose chapter is not paginated yet, so wait with `WaitForNavigationTargetAsync` first. `Pagination` completes when every page is known, faults with the error that stopped pagination, and is canceled when `ReleaseLayout` runs first. Call `ReleaseLayout` when the book closes so queued layout work stops. Page text stays readable afterward, but a page that would need layout again is refused. The token you pass to the open call cancels only the first chapter.\n\n## Read text, outlines, and links in EPUB and DjVu\n\n```csharp\nPdfReadTextPage text = book.ReadTextPage(0);\nConsole.WriteLine(text.Text);\nforeach (PdfReadTextRegion region in text.Regions)\n    Console.WriteLine($\"{region.Start}, {region.Length}: {region.Bounds}\");\n\nforeach (PdfReadNavigationEntry entry in book.ReadNavigation())\n    Console.WriteLine($\"{entry.Label}: {entry.Target}\");\n\nforeach (PdfReadLinkRegion link in book.ReadLinkRegions(0))\n    Console.WriteLine($\"{link.Target}: {link.Bounds}\");\n```\n\nText regions map a UTF-16 range of the page text to bounds in points, with the origin at the lower left, before the page's intrinsic rotation. `ReadPageText` returns a page's text without its regions. For EPUB it reads from the book's page index and never lays the chapter out again.\n\n| Capability | EPUB | DjVu |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Metadata | Title, identifier, language, and creator | Page size, resolution, and orientation |\n| Page text | Yes, with regions | Yes, with regions when the page has located text |\n| Outline | EPUB 3 navigation document, with the NCX as a fallback, or the EPUB 2 NCX | Bookmarks stored in the document |\n| Link regions | Yes, for linked text | No. `ReadLinkRegions` throws `NotSupportedException` |\n\n`ReadNavigation` returns each outline entry's label, an opaque target string, and its children. Nothing is fetched or executed. `ResolveNavigationTarget` turns a target into a page index and a position:\n\n- An EPUB target is a package-relative chapter path with an optional fragment, such as `EPUB/chapter12.xhtml#start`. Targets and fragments are case-sensitive.\n- A DjVu target must begin with `#` and name a page by component ID, component name, component title, one-based page number, or a relative offset such as `+2` or `-1`. A relative offset needs the `currentPageIndex` argument. External resources and viewer commands throw `NotSupportedException`.\n\nA DjVu destination always has a left and top of zero. A DjVu page can carry link and highlight annotations, but the engine does not read them as link regions and they do not change the rendered pixels.\n\n## Render native pages and regions\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Rendering;\n\nvar render = new PdfRenderOptions(794, 1123);\nPdfRenderedFrame frame = book.RenderPage(0, render);\nbyte[] bgra = frame.ToSrgbBgra32();\n\nPdfRenderedFrame visible = book.RenderPageRegion(0, render, left: 100, top: 200, width: 256, height: 256);\nPdfRenderedFrame natural = scan.RenderPage(0);\n```\n\nNative rendering returns a `PdfRenderedFrame`, the same float32 frame type as the precision renderer for PDF. `RenderPage(pageIndex)` draws a page at its natural size with any intrinsic rotation applied. The overload that takes `PdfRenderOptions` draws it at an exact pixel size. EPUB text is not reflowed for that size, and its glyph outlines are scaled.\n\n`RenderPageRegion` draws the pixel rectangle at `left` and `top` of a page drawn at the full size in the options. It returns exactly that crop, and only the region counts against the storage limits. A viewer can use it to draw the visible part of a page sharply at any zoom. A DjVu page is decoded once into its own compact form, and only the requested pixels are resampled from it, so 300 and 600 dpi scans render whole, resized, and by region.\n\nNative renderers accept `PdfRenderOptions.Proof` and apply it as described in the proofing section below. They refuse `SeparationPlate` with `NotSupportedException`.\n\n## Convert EPUB and DjVu to PDF\n\nConversion is always explicit. Opening a document never produces a PDF.\n\n```csharp\nbyte[] epubPdf = epub.ConvertToPdf(new EpubPdfConversionOptions(font)\n{\n    PageWidthPoints = 612,\n    PageHeightPoints = 792,\n    MarginPoints = 54,\n    IsolateChapterFailures = true,\n    DiagnosticReported = notice => Console.WriteLine($\"{notice.Reason}: {notice.Message}\")\n});\n\nvar notices = new List<DjvuDiagnostic>();\nbyte[] djvuPdf = djvu.ConvertToPdf(notices);\nforeach (DjvuDiagnostic notice in notices)\n    Console.WriteLine($\"{notice.PageIndex}: {notice.Code}\");\n```\n\nEPUB conversion takes paper size, margin, and fallback font size in points, and defaults to A4 paper. It keeps selectable text in the fonts the layout used, local and web links, PNG and JPEG images, solid backgrounds, and a bookmark hierarchy from the book's outline. Paper must be 3 to 14,400 points on each side, and `EpubPdfConversionOptions.FitsPdfPageLimit` checks that before you convert. `DiagnosticReported` receives each chapter, resource, or navigation item the conversion left out.\n\nDjVu conversion handles bitonal, grayscale, color photo, and compound pages. It writes located page text as an invisible, selectable layer and local bookmarks as PDF bookmarks. Color photo pages convert without loss unless you set `DjvuPdfConversionOptions.JpegEncoder` to a `PdfJpegEncoder` from `KillerPdf.Engine.Writing`, and `JpegQuality` (1 through 100, default 98) then applies. DjVu conversion does not write links.\n\n## What the native readers ignore, replace, or refuse\n\n**EPUB** ignores some content, replaces some with a notice, and refuses the rest:\n\n- CSS declarations and rules the reader does not implement are ignored. `PdfReadSnapshot.IgnoredDeclarations` counts them for each chapter.\n- A single image or SVG drawing that the reader cannot use is left out, and the chapter still lays out. The omission appears in `PdfReadSnapshot.ContentDiagnostics` with an `EpubOmissionReason` and the item's package path. Only PNG and JPEG images draw. A PNG or JPEG with a color profile other than sRGB, an image that is too large or damaged, and an SVG that is more than a static cover are left out. A link to a target that is not in the book stays inert.\n- A character that no available font maps draws the font's missing-glyph box and adds a content diagnostic.\n- A chapter whose content is refused ends layout and conversion with an exception. Set `IsolateChapterFailures` in `EpubLayoutOptions` or `EpubPdfConversionOptions` to replace that chapter with one page that states `UnavailableChapterLabel`, the chapter's path, and the reason, and the rest of the book lays out. `PdfReadSnapshot.ChapterDiagnostics` lists each replaced chapter. The reasons include encryption, scripting, text that needs shaping or bidirectional processing the reader does not implement, layout constructs it does not implement, safety limits, and invalid markup.\n- Package problems always refuse the book: a first ZIP entry that is not an uncompressed `mimetype` entry holding `application/epub+zip`, a missing container or package document, a package version other than 2 or 3, unsafe or ambiguous resource paths, and any exceeded package limit.\n- A package file larger than 8 MiB expanded, or one that cannot be read, is left out, and conversion reports it through `DiagnosticReported`. A chapter file in that state is refused like any other refused chapter.\n\n**DjVu** reports what it cannot do through stable notice codes:\n\n- `DjvuDocument.Diagnostics` and `PdfReadSnapshot.DjvuDiagnostics` list notices from opening, such as `ExternalComponentsUnsupported`, `IncludedComponentUnsupported`, `PageVersionUnsupported`, `ImageDecodingUnsupported`, and `UnrecognizedChunkSkipped`. Components outside a bundled document are never fetched.\n- Text that is not valid UTF-8 reads with one replacement character for each unreadable sequence, and a `TextEncodingInvalid` notice names the page. `PdfReadTextPage.Diagnostics` carries it.\n- A page the renderer cannot draw throws `NotSupportedException`. This covers pages that use JPEG layers, striped MMR masks, or a page version newer than 0.26. Embedded thumbnails are not decoded.\n- During conversion, a page that cannot be converted becomes a 612 by 792 point page that states `Page N could not be converted` and the reason, and a `PageNotConverted` notice with the page index is added. Page positions stay aligned with the source.\n- During conversion, page text that cannot be placed is left out with a `TextNotConverted` notice and the page image is kept. Characters the text layer cannot represent are written as replacement characters with a `TextCharactersReplaced` notice. A bookmark that names no page is left out with a `BookmarkNotConverted` notice, and its children move up a level.\n- A container with no local pages, such as an indirect multi-file document, makes `PdfReadSnapshot.OpenDjvu` throw `NotSupportedException`. A file that is not a DjVu container, or whose structure is invalid, throws `InvalidDataException`.\n\nFull format coverage remains in progress for both readers. Treat each diagnostic as a statement about the source file, and check the diagnostics before you rely on a conversion.\n\n## Native format limits\n\nThe readers enforce these limits in code and refuse or report input that exceeds them. An EPUB resource larger than 8 MiB expanded is left out instead of refusing the book.\n\n| Limit | EPUB | DjVu |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Input size | 128 MiB package, and 256 MiB expanded in total | 64 MiB |\n| Container entries | 4,096 ZIP entries, 4,096 manifest items, and 4,096 spine items | Not applicable |\n| Page size | 32,768 CSS pixels on a side, and at most 4,194,304 pixels | 100 million pixels per page |\n| Pages | 10,000 per book | 4,096 per document |\n| Text | 16,000,000 laid-out characters per book | 4 MiB and 65,536 located zones per page |\n| Outline | 16,384 entries, 32 levels deep | 1,024 bookmarks, 32 levels deep |\n| Navigation target | 4,096 characters | 4,096 characters |\n\n`EpubLayoutOptions.FitsPageLimits` checks a page size, margin, and font size before you lay out. `EpubLayoutOptions.TableFlowWorkLimit` sets the per-chapter table work budget from 1 through 16,000,000, and the default is 1,000,000. A DjVu page whose resolution is outside 25 to 6,000 dpi is read as 300 dpi. `PdfReadSnapshot.OpenDjvu` refuses input over 64 MiB, and `DjvuDocument.RenderPage` at natural size is limited to a 128 MiB float frame, which is 8,388,608 pixels. Render a larger page with a `PdfRenderOptions` size or by region instead.\n\n## Render with float32 precision and color management\n\n`PdfPageRenderer.RenderFrame` and `RenderFrameRegion` render a page into a `PdfRenderedFrame`: tightly packed float32 RGBA with straight alpha and a top-left origin. RGB channels are relative to document white at one, with no implied absolute luminance, and finite values outside zero through one are kept until you convert the frame for output.\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.ColorManagement;\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Rendering;\n\nvar renderer = new PdfPageRenderer(document);\nPdfRenderedFrame frame = renderer.RenderFrame(0, new PdfRenderOptions(1024, 768));\n\nbyte[] srgb = frame.ToSrgbBgra32();\n\nPdfColorProfile monitor = PdfColorProfile.Load(File.ReadAllBytes(\"monitor.icc\"));\nbyte[] display = frame.ToDisplayBgra32(monitor);\n\nHalf[] half = frame.ToPremultipliedLinearHalfRgba();\n```\n\nA frame converts to sRGB bytes, to bytes in an RGB monitor profile, or to premultiplied linear half-precision values. `ToDisplayBgra32` needs a monitor profile with an actual relative colorimetric reverse transform. Its output channels are monitor device values, not sRGB, so use it only when your application owns monitor conversion.\n\nThe float renderer does not approximate. A page that uses a feature it cannot draw with full precision throws `NotSupportedException` instead of returning quantized pixels, so catch that exception and fall back to `PdfPageRenderer.Render` when a bounded BGRA32 image is enough.\n\nColor in float frames follows these rules:\n\n- Untagged DeviceCMYK converts through the render's working CMYK profile. Tagged CMYK, a resource `DefaultCMYK`, and a usable document output intent take priority over it.\n- Float frames and printer proofs simulate overprint. A page of unprofiled DeviceCMYK fills and strokes keeps native ink plates for overprint modes 0 and 1, opacity, and proofing. A printer proof of any other overprint page composes its inks, spot plates included, and converts the composed ink. A float render that is not a proof refuses other overprinted paint.\n\n## Proof for a printer\n\nSoft proofing shows how a page looks on a printer by converting each color through the printer's ICC profile. Set `PdfRenderOptions.Proof` and render a float frame.\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Authoring;\n\nPdfColorProfile printer = PdfColorProfile.Load(File.ReadAllBytes(\"printer.icc\"));\nvar settings = new PdfDocumentProofSettings(\n    enabled: true,\n    printerProfile: printer,\n    options: new PdfProofOptions\n    {\n        RenderingIntent = PdfRenderingIntent.RelativeColorimetric,\n        BlackPointCompensation = true,\n        SimulatePaper = false,\n        SimulateBlack = false\n    },\n    gamutWarnings: true);\n\nvar proofOptions = new PdfRenderOptions(1024, 768) { Proof = settings };\nPdfRenderedFrame proof = renderer.RenderFrame(0, proofOptions);\n```\n\n`PdfProofOptions` selects the conversion:\n\n- `RenderingIntent` is `Perceptual`, `RelativeColorimetric`, `Saturation`, or `AbsoluteColorimetric`. The default is `RelativeColorimetric`.\n- `BlackPointCompensation` defaults to true.\n- `SimulatePaper` simulates the printer's paper. It defaults to false.\n- `SimulateBlack` preserves the printer's black instead of normalizing it. It defaults to false. With `SimulatePaper` on and `SimulateBlack` off, the proof keeps the paper tint and maps printer black to display black. That is a viewing aid and not an ICC rendering intent.\n\nA proof request that the engine cannot honor throws `NotSupportedException`. The printer profile must be an output (`prtr`) profile with actual colorimetric tables in both directions. The selected intent needs an actual inverse table, not a fallback to another intent. Absolute colorimetric and paper simulation also need a profile with a valid media white point. Proofing is refused when `ForPrinting` is set, and byte rendering does not apply it. EPUB and DjVu frames from `PdfReadSnapshot` accept the same proof settings.\n\n## Warn about out-of-gamut colors\n\nWith `gamutWarnings` on, the frame carries a separate `PdfGamutWarningMask` that classifies each pixel as `Inside`, `Boundary`, `Outside`, or `NotEvaluated` without changing the color pixels. A fully transparent pixel is not evaluated.\n\n```csharp\nPdfGamutWarningMask? mask = proof.PrinterGamutWarnings;\nConsole.WriteLine(mask?.WarningPixelCount);\n\nPdfRenderedFrame marked = proof.WithGamutWarningsMarked();\nbyte[] pixels = marked.ToSrgbBgra32();\n```\n\n`WithGamutWarningsMarked` returns a copy with the `Outside` pixels painted flat neutral gray (sRGB 128 of 255). It keeps alpha, every other pixel, and the mask, and it returns the same frame when nothing is outside. The marked copy is for viewing and must not be used as output. The check measures the printer tables' own round-trip error, so a table-based CMYK profile does not warn about colors it can print, and it follows the proof's black-point compensation. `PdfPrinterGamutCheck` classifies individual D50 XYZ colors against a printer profile.\n\n`PdfProofPreset` captures a proof's choices as a named, immutable preset that refers to the profile by its SHA256 content digest. It does not contain the profile. `ToJson` and `FromJson` save and load it, and `ResolveProfile` combines a preset with a profile you load, which must match the digest. `PdfLogicalDocument.Proof` holds the proof settings of a document, and `PdfDocumentProofView.ComparisonOverride` lets one view compare with different settings.\n\n## Choose the working CMYK profile\n\nUntagged DeviceCMYK needs an assumed profile. The engine bundles `CGATS001Compat-v2-micro`, a CC0 profile for CGATS TR 001 (North American web printing). `PdfCmykViewingDefault.LoadProfile` returns an owned copy of it. This is an assumption for viewing and is not a printer profile selection.\n\n```csharp\nPdfColorProfile workingCmyk = PdfColorProfile.Load(File.ReadAllBytes(\"working-cmyk.icc\"));\nvar options = new PdfRenderOptions(1024, 768) { WorkingCmykProfile = workingCmyk };\nPdfRenderedFrame frame = renderer.RenderFrame(0, options);\n\nConsole.WriteLine(frame.AssumedCmykProfileSha256);\nforeach (string note in frame.Diagnostics)\n    Console.WriteLine(note);\n```\n\nThe replacement must be a CMYK profile with a perceptual or relative colorimetric forward table, or the property setter throws. The renderer uses the perceptual table when the profile has one. A frame reports the SHA256 of the profile it assumed in `AssumedCmykProfileSha256`, which is null when the page painted no untagged CMYK, and describes the assumption in `Diagnostics`.\n\n`PdfColorProfile.Load` reads ICC version 2 and 4 input, display, output, and color-space profiles of up to 32 MiB without touching external resources. `GetTransformSelection` reports which table an intent and direction will use, and whether it falls back to another intent. `ToXyz`, `FromXyz`, and `ConvertTo` convert color components through D50 XYZ.\n\n## Know the color limits\n\nHDR output and physical color accuracy are still in progress. The engine does not claim either. A float frame is relative to document white, and its numbers are not measured luminance. `ToPremultipliedLinearHalfRgba` copies a linear frame to half-precision values for a host that presents them. It does not make a render HDR.\n\n## Know the boundary\n\nKillerPDF 1.9 uses The KillerPDF.Engine for rendering, text extraction, and OCR. Tesseract remains available in the desktop app as a fallback OCR provider. See the developer guide for current engine APIs.\n","signing-encryption":"# Signing and encryption\n\nThe engine supports password and certificate-recipient security, detached CMS signatures, RFC 3161 timestamps, certification policies, field locks, signature discovery, cryptographic verification, trust and revocation policy, and signed revision analysis.\n\n## Create a password-protected document\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Authoring;\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Security;\n\nvar encryption = new PdfPasswordEncryptionOptions\n{\n    UserPassword = \"reader password\",\n    OwnerPassword = \"owner password\",\n    AllowContentCopying = false,\n    AllowDocumentModification = false,\n    AllowHighQualityPrinting = true\n};\n\nbyte[] protectedPdf = new PdfDocumentBuilder()\n    .SetPasswordEncryption(encryption)\n    .AddBlankPage()\n    .Build();\n```\n\nNew documents use PDF 2.0 AES-256 protection, and `Aes256Gcm` selects revision 7. The engine can also read supported RC4 and AES-128 documents and preserve authenticated security during incremental updates and rewrites.\n\n## Protect for certificate recipients\n\n```csharp\nusing System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates;\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Authoring;\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Security;\n\nbyte[] protectedPdf = new PdfDocumentBuilder()\n    .SetCertificateEncryption(new PdfCertificateEncryptionOptions\n    {\n        Recipients = [recipientCertificate]\n    })\n    .AddBlankPage()\n    .Build();\n```\n\nA recipient opens the file with `PdfDocument.Open(bytes, certificateWithPrivateKey)`. To protect an existing document, use `PdfDocumentEncryptionWriter`, which rewrites it with AES-256 passwords or certificate encryption, replaces any source encryption, and refuses a signed document unless you allow signature invalidation.\n\n## Open encrypted input\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Documents;\n\nPdfDocument document = PdfDocument.Open(source, password);\nConsole.WriteLine(document.PasswordAuthenticationRole);\nConsole.WriteLine(document.DeclaredPermissions?.AllowContentCopying);\n```\n\nHonor the authenticated permission set in your application workflow. An owner password grants unrestricted access. A user password may limit printing, copying, assembly, forms, annotations, or general modification.\n\n## Sign with detached CMS\n\n`PdfDetachedSignatureWriter` prepares the PDF signature revision and calls your application back to produce the detached CMS value. Your application owns certificate selection, private-key access, and trust configuration. Set `RequestTimestamp` on `PdfSignatureOptions` to have the engine request an RFC 3161 timestamp from `TimestampServerUrl`, or from a caller-supplied `TimestampTransport`, and verify the token before embedding it. Signing keeps the source cross-reference format and can place a visible appearance on an existing signature widget.\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Signing;\n\nvar options = new PdfSignatureOptions\n{\n    FieldName = \"Signature1\",\n    SignerName = \"Example signer\",\n    Reason = \"Approved\",\n    DigestMethod = PdfSignatureDigestMethod.Sha256\n};\n```\n\nUse the `Sign` overload that matches your certificate and CMS provider. Do not copy a private key into the PDF or into application logs.\n\n## Read and verify signatures\n\n```csharp\nIReadOnlyList<PdfSignatureInfo> signatures = PdfSignatureReader.Read(document);\n\nforeach (PdfSignatureInfo signature in signatures)\n{\n    PdfSignatureVerificationResult integrity =\n        PdfSignatureVerifier.VerifyIntegrity(document, signature);\n    Console.WriteLine(integrity.IsCryptographicallyValid);\n}\n```\n\nIntegrity verification answers whether the signed bytes match the signature. Trust verification additionally evaluates the certificate chain under the trust policy supplied by your application.\n\n`PdfSignatureInspection.Inspect` combines these results in one report. It gives an explicit complete, incomplete, or invalid status, the signer name, signing time, reason, location, and contact, certificate identity and fingerprints, permissions, later-revision analysis, and PAdES validation evidence. Pass `PdfSignatureTrustOptions` to control trust anchors, revocation checking, verification time, and offline policy. Document timestamps are verified for integrity but not created by the engine.\n\n## Analyze later revisions\n\n`PdfSignedRevisionAnalyzer` identifies changes after a signature and evaluates them against certification permissions and field locks. This is different from a simple cryptographic pass or fail result. A signature can remain cryptographically intact while later revisions add changes that violate the declared policy.\n\n## Security boundaries\n\n- Treat PDF passwords as sensitive data and avoid retaining them longer than needed.\n- Keep private keys in the operating system certificate store, hardware, or another protected signing provider.\n- Make trust policy explicit. Do not treat any embedded certificate as trusted by default.\n- Validate signed output with an independent CMS implementation before release.\n","validation":"# Validation\n\nValidation is part of the engine design, not a label added after writing. Use structural inspection before editing, round-trip validation for deterministic rewrites, and independent tools for standards claims.\n\n## Inspect unfamiliar input\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Diagnostics;\n\nPdfInspectionReport report = PdfDocumentInspector.Inspect(source);\n\nif (!report.IsStructurallyValid)\n{\n    foreach (PdfDiagnostic diagnostic in report.Diagnostics)\n        Console.WriteLine($\"{diagnostic.Severity}: {diagnostic.Message}\");\n}\n```\n\nInspection is bounded and non-throwing for structural failures. It checks the header, revision chain, cross-reference data, indirect objects, catalog, object limits, and authentication state.\n\n## Validate a deterministic rewrite\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Validation;\n\nPdfRoundTripResult result = PdfRoundTripValidator.Validate(source);\nif (!result.Succeeded)\n    throw new InvalidOperationException(result.FailureMessage);\n\nFile.WriteAllBytes(\"validated.pdf\", result.RewrittenBytes!);\n```\n\nThe round-trip validator inspects the source, rewrites it, reopens the result, writes it a second time, and compares the two outputs. Use `ValidateAuthenticated` for password-protected input.\n\n## Run preflight and report checks\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerPdf.Engine.Diagnostics;\n\nPdfPreflightReport report = PdfPreflightRunner.Run(\n    source, PdfPreflightProfile.PrintProduction);\n\nif (!report.Passed || !report.Complete)\n    Console.WriteLine(report.ToText());\n```\n\nA preflight profile selects checks for structure, metadata, language, tagging, forms, links, page boxes, output intents, images, fonts, transparency, color and ink limits, layers, and declared PDF/A, PDF/UA, and PDF/X boundaries. Reports identify source objects and checks that could not run. `PdfAccessibilityInspector` and `PdfPrintProductionReport` produce focused reports, and correction plans preview a change before you apply it. A clean report is not certification.\n\n## Understand what this proves\n\n| Check | What it establishes |\n| --- | --- |\n| Structural inspection | Required PDF structure can be resolved within explicit limits |\n| Reopen after writing | The engine can parse the output it produced |\n| Second deterministic write | Equivalent input produces identical normalized output |\n| Independent validation | A separate implementation accepts the claimed structure or profile |\n\n> A successful engine round trip does not replace veraPDF, qpdf, a signature verifier, or application-specific acceptance tests.\n\n## Validation\n\nFor exact source and test counts, see the Overview. Corpus results identify the tested build and inputs. qpdf, veraPDF and OpenSSL provide separate structural, standards and signature checks.\n\n[Overview](index.html) | [Corpus](../corpus.html)\n\n## Validate the profile you claim\n\nUse veraPDF for PDF/A and PDF/UA profiles, qpdf for general structural checks, and a cryptographic implementation independent of the engine for signature interoperability. Keep the exact validator version and profile in your build or release record.\n"};})();
